MENTOR: Wipe it is. It has one linear pair: **Position from 0.6 s to 1.6 s**. For that whole second it moves at the same speed, then stops abruptly at 1.6 s.

This is the comp at 1.1 s, halfway through that move. Wipe doesn't show up on its own in this frame, so I can't point to it in the picture. If it's working as a matte, you'll see its motion in the layer it reveals, not in Wipe itself. Scrub from 0.6 s to 1.6 s and you'll notice the abrupt start and the hard stop.

Easing fixes that: the motion speeds up gently out of the first key and slows down into the last one.

Wipe has only that one pair, so I won't demo on it. If I eased it, there'd be nothing left for you to do. Here are the steps instead:

1. Select **Wipe** in the timeline and press **U** to show its keyframes.
2. Select both Position keys, at 0.6 s and 1.6 s.
3. Press **F9**, or go to **Animation > Keyframe Assistant > Easy Ease**. The diamond keys will turn into hourglass shapes.
4. To check it, open the **Graph Editor** (the graph icon above the layers). The speed should now curve up and back down instead of staying flat.

Give it a try and say **done** when you're ready. If you'd like to watch a demo first, Leaf 2 has enough pairs for me to show you one.
